{"content":{"result":[{"rank_order":1,"title":"Amazon Route 53 Resolver now supports AWS PrivateLink - AWS","context":"Amazon Route 53 Resolver now supports AWS PrivateLink, enabling private access to Route 53 Resolver and its related features, including Resolver endpoints, Route 53 Resolver DNS Firewall, Resolver Query Logging, and Resolver for AWS Outposts. This private connectivity is provided through AWS PrivateLink, which securely connects VPCs, AWS services, and on-premises applications over the Amazon network. Customers can manage these operations, such as creating, deleting, editing, and listing, through the Amazon private network. Route 53 Resolver is available in all VPCs by default and responds recursively to DNS queries for public records, Amazon VPC-specific DNS names, and Amazon Route 53 private hosted zones. The service offers additional features that can be opted into. Route 53 Resolver and its features are available in regions where the service is available today, including AWS GovCloud (US) Regions. For more information about the availability of Route 53 Resolver and its features, please refer to the service documentation","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2026/10/amazon-route53-resolver-supports-aws-privatelink/"},{"rank_order":2,"title":"Amazon Route 53 Resolver now supports AWS PrivateLink - AWS","context":"Amazon Route 53 Resolver now supports AWS PrivateLink, enabling customers to access and manage Route 53 Resolver and its related features privately without using the public internet. AWS PrivateLink provides secure connectivity between VPCs, AWS services, and on-premises applications. Operations such as creating, deleting, editing, and listing can be handled via the Amazon private network. Route 53 Resolver responds recursively to DNS queries from AWS resources for public records, Amazon VPC-specific DNS names, and Amazon Route 53 private hosted zones. It is available by default in all VPCs and offers features like Resolver endpoints, Route 53 Resolver DNS Firewall, Resolver Query Logging, and Resolver for AWS Outposts. Route 53 Resolver and its features can be used with AWS PrivateLink in regions where they are available, including AWS GovCloud (US) Regions. For more information about the availability of Route 53 Resolver and its features, refer to the service documentation","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2025/10/amazon-route53-resolver-supports-aws-privatelink/"},{"rank_order":3,"title":"route53","skill_description":"Configures Amazon Route 53 DNS: public and private records, traffic-steering routing policies, health checks, DNS Firewall, Route 53 Profiles, VPC Resolver (also known as Route 53 Resolver) for hybrid and Outposts networks, and Global Resolver. Applicable when the customer wants to point a hostname at a target, split or fail over traffic across endpoints, monitor an endpoint, block malicious domains, centralize DNS across accounts, or resolve private DNS across a hybrid network. Routes to the right per-task procedure in references. Does not cover CloudFront-specific setup or non-DNS networking.\n\n\nServices: route53, vpc, outposts, cloudwatch, sns\nTasks: configure, secure, monitor, deploy\nPersona: developer, devops, network-admin, security-engineer, sre\nWorkload: networking, security, hybrid, disaster-recovery\n*","skill_name":"route53"},{"rank_order":4,"title":"Amazon VPC Lattice resource configurations now support private domain-name targets - AWS","context":"Amazon VPC Lattice has updated its resource configurations to allow for private domain-name targets. This feature enables secure cross-account access to privately-hosted resources using a private FQDN. Previously, only publicly resolvable domain-name targets could be shared using resource configurations. To utilize this new functionality, set the 'Resource Config DNS Resolution' property to 'IN_VPC' on your resource gateway. VPC Lattice will then use your VPC's DNS configuration to resolve FQDNs and route traffic accordingly, eliminating the need for public DNS entries. This feature can be enabled through the AWS Management Console, AWS CLI, AWS SDKs, and AWS APIs, and is available at no additional cost in all AWS Regions where VPC Lattice is available. For more information, refer to the VPC Lattice user guide","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2026/05/amazon-vpc-lattice/"},{"rank_order":5,"title":"aws-networking","skill_description":"Routes AWS networking requests to the correct service skill for implementation. Covers Route 53 (DNS, health checks, routing policies, Resolver, DNS Firewall), CloudFront (caching, edge, OAC, mTLS, signed URLs), Transit Gateway (multi-VPC hub, segmentation, centralized egress), Direct Connect (hybrid link, DX Gateway, MACsec), Site-to-Site VPN (IPsec tunnels, static or BGP), WAF (web ACLs, AWS Managed Rules, rate-based rules, Bot and Fraud Control), and Shield Advanced (L3/L4 DDoS). Applicable when creating, configuring, troubleshooting, or designing across these services, choosing between them, or diagnosing connectivity or traffic-filtering issues. Not for VPC subnets and route tables, load balancers, VPC endpoints, PrivateLink, API Gateway, IAM policy logic, container or serverless networking, or IaC authoring.\n\nServices: route53, route53resolver, cloudfront, directconnect, transitgateway, vpn, waf, shield\nTasks: configure, troubleshoot, debug, deploy, secure, migrate\nPersona: developer, network-engineer, devops, security-engineer\nWorkload: networking, security\n*","skill_name":"aws-networking"}]}}"}]}], "label": "Verify whether VPC Flow Logs capture DNS traffic to the Amazon VPC resolver."}
